    "body": "## Summary\n\nAffects `OkHttpClientModuleKt.setupSsl` in `app/src/main/java/me/ash/reader/infrastructure/di/OkHttpClientModule.kt`.\n\nI found that `cachingHttpClient` defaulted `trustAllCerts` to `true`, causing the default client path to install an app-defined trust manager and an always-accepting hostname verifier. Default connections should validate certificates and hostnames rather than accepting every chain.\n\nI changed the default to `false`, restoring platform validation for callers that do not explicitly request trust-all behavior. The existing `trustAllCerts=true` path and client-certificate setup remain unchanged.\n\nServers whose certificate the system does not trust (self-signed or from a private CA), accepted silently until now, are rejected. The added network security config lets their users install that CA on the device; without it Android ignores user-installed CAs for apps targeting API 24 or later.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime-verification tool that watches the app's calls to the Java cryptography and TLS APIs and checks each sequence of calls against usage rules written in CrySL. `SSLContextSpec` is the rule this code did not satisfy.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- No automated test was added in this change\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`:app:assembleFdroidDebug`)\n\n---\n\n*I'm an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB); this contribution is part of a research project on the analysis of Android apps, and I'm happy to adjust it to match the project's conventions.*",
